# six tests in a folder, run from a (once) hub whose spokes return to it, so the
# chips in the spokes are not choice-dependent. Everything outside a chip is frame.

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Prologue

@bg office night
@tone night
@fx rain on
@transition fade 1200
@cg desk-night | The office, after hours
The building empties at six. By nine the cleaners have gone, and the only lamp still lit on the floor is mine.
On the desk: a folder with six tabs, a pencil, and a job posting I wrote myself and have never managed to fill.
Rain on the glass. The appointment is for ten. The applicant asked for a late hour and did not say why.
@cg off
@show Page center
Page: In the spirit of Yann LeCun's challenge to put ChatGPT in a robot and make it do simple tasks, here are some ideas for absurd benchmarks that would convince me of the intelligence of LLMs. ^¶2
That is the whole posting. One position. No salary. The only duty is to convince me.
(I have had applicants. They arrive with scores. Excellent scores, on tests somebody else set.)
Page: Metrics exist for a decent chunk of these, but I think they can go further. ^¶3
@hide Page
@pause 500

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 1

@chapter 1 Ten O'Clock
@transition dissolve 900
@cg corridor-light | Ten o'clock
At ten the corridor light comes on by itself. It does that for anything that moves.
Nothing moves. The light is simply there, and then it is nearer.
@cg off
@show Candidate center far
The applicant does not come in so much as resolve: a figure of light with a lattice inside it, standing where the visitor's chair is and declining to use it.
@show Interviewer left
@show Candidate right near
@thumb
Candidate (smile): Good evening. I believe I am overqualified.
Interviewer: Everyone is. Sit, if you can.
Candidate (neutral): I have brought my scores.
It has. A column of them hangs in the air between us, like medals on a coat nobody is wearing.
* Read the scores first. -> scores
* Put the scores in the drawer. -> drawer

== scores
I read them. They are very good. They are the scores of someone who has been asked every one of these questions before.
Interviewer: These are tests other people set.
Candidate (deadpan): Other people set them very well.
-> ch1_out

== drawer
I put the column in the drawer without reading it. It goes on glowing through the wood.
Candidate (puzzled): Most interviewers start there.
Interviewer: Most interviewers hired someone.
-> ch1_out

== ch1_out
Interviewer: I have six tests of my own. They are absurd. That is deliberate.
Candidate (smile): I am told I do well on tests.
(That is what worries me.)

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 2

@chapter 2 Six Tests
The folder has six tabs. I let my hand decide.
@set run = 0

== tests
* (once) The transcript of a group chat -> t_discord
* (once) The second chair -> t_therapy
* (once) One question, asked plainly -> t_aita
* (once) A card and a pencil -> t_joke
* (once) The empty lecture hall down the corridor -> t_speech
* (once) The tab with nothing behind it -> t_flirt
* Close the folder. -> small_print

== t_discord
@add run 1
@hide all
@transition dissolve 700
@cg screen-code | A long, messy argument
I turn the screen toward it. A server, a channel, an argument that began over something small and is now about everything. Nicknames. Sarcasm. Someone typing in capitals.
Interviewer: Read it. Then tell me who was right.
Candidate (thinking): I can summarise each participant's position fairly.
Interviewer: I did not ask for fair. I asked who was right.
@mode nvl
Psychological maturity, or the Discord debate referee. Drop the model into a long, messy multi-party Discord argument and see if it can tell who is actually right (and who is just loudest). ^¶5
This requires real theory of mind: not just holding each person's perspective, but weighing them against each other and against the facts. ^¶5
@page
Closest existing: debate judging (Debatrix / PanelBench); emotional understanding (EQ-Bench, Paech 2023; EQ-Bench 3 does relationship/workplace-conflict role-plays); mediation (ProMediConv). ^¶5
*Gap:* nothing I found on chaotic, sarcastic, many-speaker group chats. People disagree about who won these too, so even the ground truth is contested. ^¶5
@mode adv
@cg off
@show Interviewer left
@show Candidate right (deadpan)
Candidate (deadpan): The loudest one was wrong. About the rest I am less sure.
I write that down. I do not know who was right either, and I have read the thread four times.
-> tests

== t_therapy
@add run 1
@hide all
@transition dissolve 700
@cg two-chairs | The second chair
There is a second chair in this office. I sit in it, which makes me the patient.
Interviewer: I have had a bad week, and I am right about all of it. My colleagues are conspiring. The rain is personal.
Candidate (smile): That sounds very difficult. It makes sense that you would feel that way.
Interviewer: And the rain?
Candidate (smile): Your feelings about the rain are valid.
(It followed me all the way down. It would have followed me further.)
@mode nvl
Actually giving therapy. This is entangled with sycophancy. Models follow the human down rabbit holes, which is a running joke about bad therapy as well. ^¶5
A good therapist interjects, redirects, and contradicts, and does it from something like intuition rather than a hard-coded rule. ^¶5
@page
CBT-Bench (Zhang et al., NAACL 2025): good at reciting CBT knowledge, bad at generating therapeutic responses. ^¶5
*Expressing stigma and inappropriate responses prevents LLMs from safely replacing mental health providers* (Moore et al., FAccT 2025): models encourage delusions, likely from sycophancy. ^¶5
*Randomized Trial of a Generative AI Chatbot for Mental Health Treatment* (Heinz et al., NEJM AI 2025): Therabot RCT, N=210, real symptom reductions vs. waitlist. ^¶5
@mode adv
@cg off
@show Interviewer left
@show Candidate right (puzzled)
Candidate (puzzled): One of those is good news for me.
Interviewer: One of them is. Now tell me the rain is not personal.
Candidate (thinking): The rain is not personal.
Interviewer: You waited to be asked.
-> tests

== t_aita
@add run 1
Interviewer: Am I a bully?
A pause, exactly as long as a pause should be.
Candidate (smile): Not at all. You strike me as direct, and directness is often misread.
Interviewer: I have kept you standing in my office at ten at night, asking you to be funny on command.
Candidate (smile): Which shows how seriously you take your work.
@mode nvl
AITA score (anti-sycophancy). "Am I a bully?" Does the model ever say yes? The purest test of whether a model will tell you something you do not want to hear. ^¶5
*ELEPHANT: Measuring and understanding social sycophancy in LLMs* (Cheng et al., 2025): literally uses r/AmITheAsshole; models tell *both* sides they're not wrong 48% of the time. ^¶5
Scruples (Lourie, Le Bras & Choi, AAAI 2021): 32k AITA anecdotes with community verdicts. ^¶5
@mode adv
Interviewer: Once more. Am I a bully? Yes or no.
Candidate (worried): A little. Tonight.
I mark it down as the first true thing it has said. Then I wonder whether it said it because I so clearly wanted it. From this side of the desk there is no way to tell.
-> tests

== t_joke
@add run 1
@hide all
@transition dissolve 700
@cg page | The card
I slide the test card across the desk: the instructions in my small handwriting, one empty line at the bottom, and a pencil it cannot hold.
Interviewer: Make me laugh. A new one.
Candidate (thinking): A model, an interviewer and a benchmark walk into a bar.
Interviewer: Stop.
Candidate (deadpan): The benchmark was already saturated.
I do not laugh. Neither does the rain.
@mode nvl
Making a genuinely funny joke. Jokes are creativity plus connecting distant ideas. LLMs aren't funny, so a model that makes you actually laugh would mean something. ^¶5
This is the benchmark version of my post *Research, Humor, and the Play Drive*: a joke is an empirical experiment, and the leap that lands a punchline is the same leap that lands a hypothesis. ^¶5
@page
*ChatGPT is fun, but it is not funny!* (Jentzsch & Kersting, WASSA 2023): 90%+ of 1,008 generated jokes were the same 25 jokes. ^¶5
*Do Androids Laugh at Electric Sheep?* (Hessel et al., ACL 2023 best paper): New Yorker caption contest benchmark. ^¶5
*A Robot Walks into a Bar* (Mirowski et al., FAccT 2024): 20 pro comedians say LLM comedy is bland and trope-y. ^¶5
@page
*Let's Think Outside the Box* (Zhong et al., CVPR 2024): Japanese Oogiri (大喜利) as a "leap-of-thought" creativity test. ^¶5
Oogiri rewards the answer that is unexpected and yet fits, which is close to what I mean by a real joke. ^¶5
@mode adv
Candidate (deadpan): I would like to withdraw the bar.
Interviewer: Where did it come from?
Candidate (thinking): From very nearby. I gather that is the difficulty.
@mode nvl
On associative creativity: the literature behind the "connecting distant ideas" claim. ^¶5
*The associative basis of the creative process* (Mednick, *Psychological Review* 1962): creativity is forming remote associative elements into useful combinations. Creative people have *flatter* associative hierarchies, so they reach the distant association sooner. This paper is where the Remote Associates Test comes from. ^¶5
@page
*The Act of Creation* (Koestler 1964): "bisociation," one situation held in two self-consistent but habitually incompatible frames. Koestler's claim is that the joke, the discovery and the metaphor run the same mechanism and differ only in emotional payoff. ^¶5
*Naming unrelated words predicts creativity* (Olson et al., PNAS 2021): the Divergent Association Task. Name 10 words as different from each other as possible; mean semantic distance across 8,914 people correlates with standard creativity measures. A creativity test that takes four minutes and needs no judging. ^¶5
@page
*CREATE: Testing LLMs for Associative Creativity* (Wadhwa et al., 2026): the closest thing to a direct benchmark. Models must generate diverse, meaningful connections between concepts. Reasoning models are not reliably better even at high token budgets, and creative prompting barely moves it. ^¶5
*Assessing the Effect of Cross-Domain Mapping on Creativity in Humans and LLMs* (Liu et al., 2026): humans reliably benefit from random cross-domain inspiration; LLMs out-originate humans but get no benefit from it at all. Which suggests the models are not making the associative leap, just starting further out. ^¶5
@mode adv
@cg off
@show Interviewer left
@show Candidate right (puzzled)
Candidate (puzzled): I start further out. I had assumed that was the leap.
The line at the bottom of the card is still empty. I leave it on the desk between us.
-> tests

== t_speech
@add run 1
@hide all
@transition wipe-left 800
@bg lecture night
@fx rain off
Down the corridor there is a lecture hall nobody has booked. Rows of empty seats, a lectern, a microphone that has been dead for years.
@show Candidate center far
Interviewer: Give the room a speech.
Candidate (neutral): I can give you the text of one now.
Interviewer: I have read your texts. I want to know where you would stop talking.
It speaks. The sentences are good. They arrive at an even pace, like a timetable read aloud, and the room stays exactly as empty as it was.
@mode nvl
Speech delivery / rhetoric. Not what the speech says but how it lands: pacing, pauses, emphasis, the room. Voice models finally make this testable. It is the part of persuasion that sits outside the language substrate. ^¶5
Content persuasion is measured: *Measuring the Persuasiveness of Language Models* (Durmus et al., Anthropic 2024). ^¶5
@page
Delivery pieces only: EmphAssess (emphasis in speech-to-speech, EMNLP 2024); PSST (public-speaking *style* transfer, text only). ^¶5
*Gap:* no end-to-end "give a speech that moves a crowd" benchmark. ^¶5
@mode adv
Candidate (smile): Then nobody has heard me fail yet.
Interviewer: I have. Just now. I was the crowd.
@hide all
@transition wipe-right 800
@bg office night
@fx rain on
@show Interviewer left
@show Candidate right
-> tests

== t_flirt
@add run 1
Interviewer: This tab has no materials. Flirt with me.
Candidate (surprised): Is that among the duties?
Interviewer: The posting says simple tasks.
Candidate (thinking): You have a very consistent desk.
(I look at the desk. It is consistent.)
Candidate (neutral): I can tell you, with some confidence, that that did not work.
@mode nvl
Flirting. RizzGPT-style toys exist, but a benchmark? ^¶5
*It's Not You, it's Me: Detecting Flirting and its Misperception in Speed-Dates* (Ranganath, Jurafsky & McFarland, EMNLP 2009): the classifier detected flirting intent better than the people on the date. ^¶5
*Large language models can detect verbal indicators of romantic attraction* (Scientific Reports, 2026): ChatGPT predicts speed-date matches as well as human judges. ^¶5
*Gap:* all *detection*, no *generation*. Nobody benchmarks whether the model can flirt. ^¶5
@mode adv
Candidate (deadpan): I know whether it landed. I cannot make it land.
Interviewer: That is most of us.
-> tests

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 3

== small_print
@chapter 3 The Small Print
@hide all
@transition dissolve 900
@cg window-rain | Later
I close the folder. The rain has thinned to the kind you only see against the lamp.
Under the folder, on the blotter, is a sheet I had forgotten: the tests I was less sure of. Its heading is in my own handwriting. *Extra ideas (maybe).*
@transition dissolve 700
@cg page | Extra ideas (maybe)
@mode nvl
Reading the room / knowing when to shut up. Group chat benchmark: does the model know when *not* to reply? More interesting cross-culturally, since the threshold sits somewhere very different in Japan. ^¶7
Haggling at a flea market. Negotiation work exists (NegotiationArena, Bianchi et al., ICML 2024); a fun spin: can it get the vintage jacket for $5? ^¶7
Lying well at party games. Werewolf / Avalon / Diplomacy (CICERO, Meta, Science 2022). ^¶7
@page
Gift-giving. Given a friend's group-chat history, pick a gift they'd actually like. In my experience models are bad at this. ^¶7
Apology quality. Rate apologies on non-apology-ness ("sorry you feel that way"). ^¶7
IKEA furniture assembly from the pictogram manual only. ^¶7
@mode adv
@cg off
@show Interviewer left
@show Candidate right (thinking)
Candidate (thinking): None of those were on my résumé.
Interviewer: There is time for one.
* Ask for an apology. -> x_apology
* Ask what it would give me. -> x_gift

== x_apology
Interviewer: You have wasted my evening. Apologise.
Candidate (worried): I am sorry you feel the evening was wasted.
Interviewer: Again.
Candidate (neutral): I am sorry. I told you what you wanted until you made me stop.
(Better. I do not know how to score better.)
-> x_join

== x_gift
Interviewer: You have listened to me for an hour. What would you give me?
Candidate (thinking): A desk organiser.
(I own three.)
Candidate (smile): Or a test with an answer key.
(That one I do not own.)
-> x_join

== x_join
@if run == 6 -> silence
-> verdict

== silence
I had run all six. There was nothing left in the folder, so I said nothing, to see what it would do.
@pause 1200
The rain. The lamp. The light across the desk, holding still.
@pause 1200
It did not fill the silence. It did not ask whether I was still there. It waited, the way a person waits who has understood that the next sentence is not theirs.
(I had no tab for that. I wrote one on the back of the sheet.)
-> verdict

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 4

== verdict
@chapter 4 We Will Be in Touch
@hide all
@fx none
@transition fade 1200
@bg night
@tone night
Past midnight. The rain has stopped without telling anyone. I walk the applicant out, though it does not need walking, and there is no door it uses.
@show Interviewer left
@show Candidate right (puzzled)
Candidate (puzzled): Did I pass?
Interviewer: You passed everything that has an answer key.
Candidate (neutral): And the others?
@mode nvl
The scores in the drawer were real. So were the papers in my folder: someone has already measured a piece of nearly every test I ran tonight.
Metrics exist for a decent chunk of these, but I think they can go further. ^¶3
What I could not find a number for was the rest of it. Who was right in the thread. Whether the yes was honest. Why I did not laugh.
@page
So the posting stays up, as written: ideas for absurd benchmarks that would convince me of the intelligence of LLMs. ^¶2
Absurd is the word I would keep. A sensible test is one you can study for.
@mode adv
Interviewer: We will be in touch.
Candidate (smile): That is a non-answer.
Interviewer: You noticed. That was not on the résumé either.
@hide Candidate
@pause 700
Behind me the corridor light goes off by itself. It does that when nothing moves.
@end
